1
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:31,062
MATTHEW: Last time, in Boca Raton, a prison company bought the name of a college stadium, and the students ran it out of town in six weeks. This time we are fifty miles south. And this time it is not a name on a building.

2
00:00:31,362 --> 00:00:41,036
MATTHEW: Miami, Florida. Florida International University. The I stands for international. More than thirty-five hundred students are here on visas. About two out of three students are Hispanic.

3
00:00:41,336 --> 00:00:54,430
MATTHEW: In early 2025 the governor pressed Florida's public universities to sign up their campus police with ICE. The agreement lets a campus officer question a person about immigration status, check the federal databases, and make the arrest.

4
00:00:54,730 --> 00:00:59,121
GARY MERCER: Campus police. The people who unlock your car and write parking tickets.

5
00:00:59,421 --> 00:01:06,565
WHITEY RUIZ: Same department, Gary. The federal program is called 287(g). It deputizes local officers to do immigration work.

6
00:01:06,865 --> 00:01:13,473
MATTHEW: FIU's police applied in April 2025. That same month the school confirmed that eighteen of its students had their visas revoked.

7
00:01:13,773 --> 00:01:21,914
MATTHEW: The students came out. The chant was, paws off our students. The chair of the board said no student should be fearful if they are here legally and in compliance with the law.

8
00:01:22,214 --> 00:01:26,752
GARY MERCER: Well, he has a point. If you are legal you have got nothing to worry about.

9
00:01:27,052 --> 00:01:33,707
MATTHEW: Eighteen students had a valid visa one day and did not have one the next. Legal is a word that somebody else gets to change.

10
00:01:34,007 --> 00:01:44,807
MATTHEW: On July 2, 2025, the agreement became final. At least fifteen public colleges and universities in Florida signed one. Reporters could not find a single one in any other state.

11
00:01:45,107 --> 00:01:53,175
MATTHEW: The president said the only thing that really changed was that FIU was in control. A professor said it shorter. FIU without the I is F U.

12
00:01:53,475 --> 00:01:56,235
WHITEY RUIZ: That was Philip Stoddard, a professor here at FIU.

13
00:01:56,535 --> 00:02:12,444
MATTHEW: So the students did what the students up the road did in 2013. They organized. They called themselves ICEBreakers. In March 2026, at an event with the president, they stood up in T-shirts that said ICE off FIU. They did not say a word. It lasted under five minutes.

14
00:02:12,744 --> 00:02:24,939
MATTHEW: Seven of them were charged under the school's expressive activities policy. Each one got a written reprimand. And each one was ordered to record a two minute reflection video, or lose the right to register for class or collect a diploma.

15
00:02:25,239 --> 00:02:28,102
GARY MERCER: A reflection video. For standing up in a T-shirt.

16
00:02:28,402 --> 00:02:30,209
WHITEY RUIZ: Two minutes, due September 4.

17
00:02:30,509 --> 00:02:35,162
MATTHEW: In August they sued in federal court, with the ACLU of Florida. That case is still open.

18
00:02:35,462 --> 00:02:44,584
MATTHEW: To be fair to the school. FIU says there has not been one immigration enforcement action on its campuses since it signed. And the president says ICE would have access either way.

19
00:02:44,884 --> 00:02:54,961
MATTHEW: But put the two stories side by side. In 2013 the students said no, and the company went home and the president resigned. In 2026 the students said no, and they got homework.

20
00:02:55,261 --> 00:03:02,742
MATTHEW: Ask yourself again. Who is out of line? The kid in the T-shirt? Or the people who made the police chief a deputy and the protest a violation?

21
00:03:03,042 --> 00:03:05,288
GARY MERCER: I take it back. Both times.

22
00:03:05,588 --> 00:03:08,930
MATTHEW: We will follow the lawsuit. Miami, Florida. Keep it real.
